"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[33225],{733225:e=>{e.exports='# Info: Notable output templates\n\nA **Notable** is an alert generated when a Detection Rule matches an event; the template defines what analysts see.\n\n## Understanding Notable Output\n\nNotable output has two components:\n\n**1. Summary**: A short, dynamic headline for the alert list\n\n- Example: `Brute force attack: {fail_count} failed logins from {source_ip} targeting {user_name}`\n- Uses `{placeholder}` syntax to insert dynamic values from the SQL query\n- Appears in alert lists and dashboards\n\n**2. Context**: Key-value mappings that extract specific data fields\n\n- Maps field names to SQL column expressions\n- Example: `user_name`, `source_ip`, `fail_count`, `event_hour`\n- Every `{placeholder}` in Summary must have a corresponding Context mapping\n- Provides structured data for investigation\n\n**Important**: Field names must correspond to columns in your Detection SQL query output (use aliases from the SELECT statement).\n\n## Example: Brute Force Detection Notable Output\n\nFor a brute force detection with this SQL query:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  actor.user.name AS user_name,\n  src_endpoint.ip AS source_ip,\n  COUNT(*) AS fail_count,\n  DATE_TRUNC(\'hour\', time) AS event_hour,\n  MIN(time) AS first_seen,\n  MAX(time) AS last_seen\nFROM authentication\nWHERE status = \'Failure\'\nGROUP BY user_name, source_ip, event_hour\nHAVING fail_count > 100\n```\n\n**Summary template:**\n\n```\nBrute force attack: {fail_count} failed logins from {source_ip} targeting {user_name}\n```\n\n**Context fields:**\n\n- `user_name`: The targeted account\n- `source_ip`: The attacking IP address\n- `fail_count`: Number of failed login attempts\n- `event_hour`: When the attack occurred\n- `first_seen`: First failed login in this window\n- `last_seen`: Most recent failed login\n\n**Preview with sample data:**\n\n```\nBrute force attack: 47 failed logins from 192.168.1.100 targeting admin\n```\n\n**Why this works:**\n\n- **Specific threat type**: "Brute force attack" immediately tells analysts what they\'re looking at\n- **Key metrics**: `{fail_count}` shows attack severity\n- **Clear identifiers**: `{source_ip}` and `{user_name}` enable quick investigation\n- **Concise**: One sentence that fits in alert lists\n- **Actionable**: Analyst knows exactly what to investigate\n\n## Example: Data Exfiltration Notable Output\n\nFor a data exfiltration detection:\n\n**Summary template:**\n\n```\nLarge outbound data transfer detected from {source_ip} to {destination_ip}:{destination_port}\n```\n\n**Context fields:**\n\n- `source_ip`: Source IP address\n- `destination_ip`: Destination IP address\n- `destination_port`: Destination port number\n- `total_bytes_out`: Total bytes transferred\n- `connection_count`: Number of connections\n- `protocol`: Network protocol used\n- `first_seen`: First connection in this window\n- `last_seen`: Most recent connection\n\n**Preview:**\n\n```\nLarge outbound data transfer detected from 10.0.1.50 to 203.0.113.10:443\n```\n\n## Example: Insider Threat Notable Output\n\nFor detecting unusual file access:\n\n**Summary template:**\n\n```\n{user_name} accessed {sensitive_file_count} sensitive files in {file_category}\n```\n\n**Context fields:**\n\n- `user_name`: User who accessed files\n- `sensitive_file_count`: Number of files accessed\n- `file_category`: Category of sensitive files (e.g., "HR", "Finance")\n- `file_names`: List of accessed files\n- `access_type`: Type of access (read, download, etc.)\n- `first_access`: Time of first access\n- `last_access`: Time of last access\n\n**Preview:**\n\n```\njsmith accessed 15 sensitive files in HR records\n```\n\n## Key Concepts Explained\n\n### Placeholder syntax\n\nPlaceholders in the Summary use `{column_name}` syntax to insert dynamic values from the detection SQL query:\n\n```sql\n-- SQL query defines columns\nSELECT\n  src_endpoint.ip AS source_ip,\n  COUNT(*) AS fail_count\n```\n\n```\n-- Summary template references those columns\nBrute force: {fail_count} attempts from {source_ip}\n```\n\n```\n-- Result when match occurs\nBrute force: 47 attempts from 192.168.1.100\n```\n\n**Important:** Use the SQL column alias (e.g., `source_ip`), not the original table path (e.g., `src_endpoint.ip`).\n\n### Context field structure\n\nContext fields are key-value pairs where:\n\n- **Key**: Field name (e.g., `source_ip`)\n- **Value**: SQL expression to extract the value (typically `{column_name}`)\n\n**Example:**\n\n- Field name: `source_ip`\n- Extraction: `{source_ip}` (references SQL column)\n- Result in Notable: `source_ip: 192.168.1.100`\n\nContext fields provide structured data that:\n\n- Appears in the Notable details view\n- Can be searched and filtered\n- Enables correlation across multiple Notables\n- Supports automated response workflows\n\n### Why good summaries matter\n\nSecurity analysts review hundreds of alerts per day. The Summary is the first thing they see in alert lists and dashboards.\n\n**Good summaries enable:**\n\n- **Quick triage**: Analyst knows threat type and severity at a glance\n- **Prioritization**: Clear metrics help identify critical alerts\n- **Context switching**: Analyst can assess without clicking into details\n- **Dashboard effectiveness**: Summaries appear in monitoring views\n\n**Poor summaries cause:**\n\n- **Alert fatigue**: Generic "Alert detected" messages all look the same\n- **Slow response**: Analyst must click each alert to understand it\n- **Missed threats**: Critical alerts get lost in noise\n- **Investigation delays**: Unclear summaries require extra research\n\n### When to include which context fields\n\n**Always include:**\n\n- All placeholders from the Summary (required)\n- Primary identifiers (IP addresses, usernames, hostnames)\n- Key metrics (counts, totals, durations)\n\n**Consider including:**\n\n- Timing information (`first_seen`, `last_seen`)\n- Source and destination details\n- Categorical information (protocol, status, type)\n- Threshold values for reference\n\n**Generally exclude:**\n\n- Raw technical IDs that don\'t help investigation\n- Redundant information already in Summary\n- Large text blocks (use summary instead)\n- Constant values that never change\n\n## Best Practices\n\n### Write specific, actionable summaries\n\n✅ **Good:**\n\n- `Brute force attack: {fail_count} failed logins from {source_ip} targeting {user_name}`\n- `{user_name} accessed {file_count} sensitive files in {category}`\n- `Data exfiltration: {bytes_out} MB from {source_host} to {destination_ip}`\n\n❌ **Avoid:**\n\n- `Alert detected` (too generic)\n- `{user_name} {action} {object}` (unclear threat)\n- `Something suspicious happened with {ip}` (vague)\n\n### Keep summaries concise\n\nSummaries should fit in a single line of an alert list (typically 80-120 characters).\n\n**Guidelines:**\n\n- One sentence\n- 3-5 key pieces of information\n- Clear threat type at the start\n- Specific identifiers and metrics\n\n**If you need more detail:**\n\n- Put it in Context fields\n- Add `description` field to Context\n- Use longer explanatory field names\n\n### Match field names to SQL columns exactly\n\nContext field names must match the SQL column aliases from the detection query:\n\n```sql\n-- SQL query\nSELECT\n  src_endpoint.ip AS source_ip,\n  actor.user.name AS user_name\n```\n\n✅ **Correct:**\n\n- `source_ip: "{source_ip}"`\n- `user_name: "{user_name}"`\n\n❌ **Incorrect:**\n\n- `source_ip: "{src_endpoint.ip}"` (use alias, not table path)\n- `ip: "{source_ip}"` (key doesn\'t match column name)\n- `sourceIP: "{source_ip}"` (wrong capitalization)\n\n### Preview with sample data before proposing\n\nAlways run the detection SQL query to:\n\n- Verify which columns are available\n- Get sample data for preview\n- Validate that placeholders will work\n- Show user what the Summary will look like with real data\n\nThis helps catch errors before configuration and gives users confidence in the template.\n\n### Present in user-friendly format\n\nBy default, present Notable output configuration without YAML syntax:\n\n✅ **Good:**\n\n```\n**Proposed Summary:**\nBrute force attack: {fail_count} failed logins from {source_ip}\n\n**Preview:**\nBrute force attack: 47 failed logins from 192.168.1.100\n\n**Context Fields:**\n- source_ip: The attacking IP address\n- fail_count: Number of failed attempts\n- user_name: The targeted account\n```\n\n❌ **Avoid (unless requested):**\n\n```yaml\noutput:\n  summary: \'Brute force attack: {fail_count}...\'\n  context:\n    - key: source_ip\n      value: \'{source_ip}\'\n```\n\nOnly show YAML format if user explicitly requests it.\n\n## Common Mistakes to Avoid\n\n### ❌ Missing context mappings for placeholders\n\n**Problem:** Summary uses `{fail_count}` but Context doesn\'t include `fail_count` field.\n\n**Impact:** Notable generation fails, or placeholder shows as literal text.\n\n**Solution:** Every `{placeholder}` in Summary must have a corresponding entry in Context.\n\n### ❌ Referencing non-existent columns\n\n**Problem:** Context field references `{failed_attempts}` but SQL column is `fail_count`.\n\n**Impact:** Field extraction fails, no data appears in Notable.\n\n**Solution:** Read detection SQL carefully and use exact column names from SELECT statement. Run query to verify available columns.\n\n### ❌ Using table paths instead of aliases\n\n**Problem:** Using `{src_endpoint.ip}` instead of `{source_ip}` (the alias).\n\n**Impact:** Field extraction fails.\n\n**Solution:** Always use SQL aliases defined in the SELECT statement, not the original table.column paths.\n\n### ❌ Overly verbose summaries\n\n**Problem:** Summary is 200+ characters with too much detail.\n\n**Impact:** Doesn\'t fit in alert lists, hard to scan quickly, defeats purpose of summary.\n\n**Solution:** Keep Summary to one sentence (80-120 characters). Put additional details in Context fields.\n\n**Example:**\n❌ `Brute force attack detected on user account {user_name} with {fail_count} failed login attempts originating from source IP address {source_ip} during the time period {first_seen} to {last_seen} using protocol {protocol}`\n\n✅ `Brute force: {fail_count} failed logins from {source_ip} targeting {user_name}`\n\n### ❌ Generic, non-actionable summaries\n\n**Problem:** Summary says "Alert detected" or "Suspicious activity".\n\n**Impact:** Analyst can\'t triage without clicking into details, slows investigation, causes alert fatigue.\n\n**Solution:** Be specific about threat type and include key identifying information.\n'}}]);
